Cell[TextData[{
 "Their difference is the number of vertices of ",
 StyleBox["A", "TI"],
 " with a neighbour outside ",
 StyleBox["A", "TI"],
 ", the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraBoundary"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraBoundary", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "0f9f9635-00f3-4b03-b2ca-a16a5ccc2ffb"],
 " of ",
 StyleBox["A", "TI"],
 "; ",
 StyleBox["A\[Degree]", "TI"],
 " is its ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraInterior"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraInterior", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "1c4a46bd-76e1-447a-9851-9cc98b2f9daa"],
 ". In the limit of a manifold the boundary of a region has measure zero and \
the two agree. On a graph they do not: the boundary of a ball of radius ",
 StyleBox["r", "TI"],
 " holds a share of order ",
 StyleBox["1/r", "TI"],
 " of its vertices. The rim of a finite graph is not a boundary: a region \
that fills the graph has both measures equal to the number of vertices."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"96766cb5-e627-4f87-a532-d15db2dfd949"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For a ball or a tube the interior is one layer thinner. With ",
 StyleBox["T_s(X)", "TI"],
 " the vertices within ",
 StyleBox["s", "TI"],
 " of a set ",
 StyleBox["X", "TI"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["s \[GreaterEqual] 1", "TI"],
 ","
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"b4eb0667-ab8d-444d-b0b4-787d542e7ee5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Int T_s(X) = T_(s\[Minus]1)(X) \:222a {w : d(w, X) = s and no \
neighbour of w is at distance s + 1}", "TI"],
 ","
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"0008c321-6284-4a59-8649-6c2a17ad92a4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "so the Riemannian measure of a ball or a tube is the counting measure one \
step thinner, plus the dead ends of its outer layer. On the square and the \
triangular grids there are no dead ends away from the rim, and the Riemannian \
measure of a ball of radius ",
 StyleBox["r \[GreaterEqual] 1", "TI"],
 " is the counting measure of the ball of radius ",
 StyleBox["r \[Minus] 1", "TI"],
 ", the convention of the Wolfram Physics technical introduction; on the \
hexagonal tiling this is measured. A set every vertex of which has a \
neighbour outside has Riemannian measure ",
 Cell[BoxData["0"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "ea471f71-4ebb-4f04-9df6-7307f991a8c2"],
 ": a shell, a sphere instance, a shortest path in a graph of minimum degree \
three."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"fb2e30b9-b615-4a84-959e-6cb573bef331"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The volume profiles on the square grid, about a centre ",
 StyleBox["c", "TI"],
 ", with a segment from ",
 StyleBox["c", "TI"],
 " whose interval is a box of sides ",
 StyleBox["a_1", "TI"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["a_2", "TI"],
 ", away from the rim:"
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"259fb35b-a4b7-4765-bd65-0204c23c28be"],

